speechtext:
Under direction from the late and regretted Mr. Cardin, Minister of Public Works at the time, I had succeeded in having the Department of Public Works purchase a piece of land in Donnacona and prepare plans, and in having a credit approved in the 1939 estimates. Unfortunately, the war came and it shattered all hopes of these people to whom I owe much and who
The Budget-Mr. P. Gauthier

have a right to expect a great deal from the federal government as well as from the provincial government.
I renew this request because, since 1939, the population has increased by 1,200 which is sufficient indication that the building used for this service is entirely inadequate. The people are justifiably complaining. Allow me, Mr. Speaker, to quote the following resolution passed by the municipal council of Donnacona, which was communicated to me and of which I sent a copy to the Minister of Finance (Mr. Abbott) some time ago:
January 5, 1948.
Whereas the town of Donnacona is the seat of the county of Portneuf, province of Quebec, and is at the same time a very important industrial centre;
Whereas all the citizens of Donnacona recognize the untiring work done by Mr. Arthur Delisle, postmaster;
Whereas the premises now occupied by the post office are too small and no longer suited to the needs of an important town like Donnacona;
Whereas the whole population of Donnacona earnestly demands the building of a post office by the dominion government;
Whereas the dominion government has already purchased in Donnacona a site for the building of a post office;
Whereas the dominion government has already recognized the necessity of a post office at Donnacona;
Whereas Donnacona requested the building of a post office six years ago;
Whereas the said post office is now urgently needed;
It is moved by Alderman Isidore Dore, seconded by Alderman Elisee Veillette and unanimously carried that the dominion government, through the Post Office Department, be entreated to accede to the request submitted by the citizens of Donnacona regarding the erection of a post office to meet the needs of the people.
I have been urged by the citizens of Donnacona to renew the request I made some time ago to the Minister of Finance. I hope that his supplementary estimates will include an appropriation of at least $35,000, because the plans drafted in 1939 are now inadequate and will have to be revised to cope with the mail traffic at Donnacona. I said, a while ago, that the people of Donnacona deserve it. They give the finest example in the whole country of the co-operation that should exist between labour and industry. In that district, where strikes are unknown, grievances are settled through discussion, thanks to the perfect agreement that exists between industrialists as well as civil and religious authorities. If this example were followed all over the country in every district and community, there would be no cause to grieve over long work stoppages that entail heavy financial losses to industry and labour, but more especially to the workers' families.

I now come to my second request. I shall state it more briefly because the plans are not yet complete. I have in mind the building of a wharf on the St. Lawrence just opposite the picturesque parish of Cap Sante, perhaps one of the most attractive tourist haunts in the constituency of Portneuf, along with Descham-bault and Neuville.
I wrote to the Minister of Public Works (Mr. Fournier) stating my reasons for requesting the building of a wharf. I do not need to restate them in the house. I trust however the Minister of Finance will as usual have the patience of an angel and kindly listen to the claims of the member for Portneuf. They are few but he has them at heart. They are submitted with the best intentions. A friend of mine, the hon. member for Outremont (Mr. Rinfret) has reminded me of a request I had forgotten. I thank him for it. On behalf of all the farmers in my constituency and throughout the province, I ask the Minister of Finance to consider the case of those farmers compelled, as you know, to pay income tax.
I would ask the hon. Minister of Finance and the hon. Minister of National Revenue (Mr. McCann) if they could not find one way of reducing the income tax in the case of farmers. Let me explain why I make that request. The farmer employs on his land one, two or even three of his sons to whom, quite often, he pays no salary. I would ask the Minister of Finance and the Minister of National Revenue if it would not be possible to deduct from the farmer's taxable income a certain amount in lieu of such salaries in order to allow him, and especially his song, to work with greater heart, to make the land more fruitful, and so to enable them to develop and enrich the heritage which was entrusted to them. They will be able in this way to keep more money at home, if possible, which will benefit the family and the young people.
I know that it is often stated that the federal government pays for family allowances. Had I only had the time I would have put on record the moneys which it provides in grants and subsidies of all sorts to the farmers of Quebec alone. Unfortunately, eleven o'clock is coming on and I see that my time is almost up. I have no intention of uselessly lengthening this debate.
I would therefore ask that special consideration be given to those farmers who employ their sons on their land, from, shall we say, sixteen to eighteen or nineteen, in order to help develop the national family heritage, and in order also to encourage those farmers' sons to stay on the land, to help, for several years, their parents, their younger brothers

The Budget-Mr. P. Gauthier
and sisters, so that greater sunshine may yet flood this good Canadian earth to which the farmer is so atta'hed and for which he has given so freely of his sweat and oftentimes, of his blood.
Mr. Speaker, I often regret that our hon. friends opposite do not read the history of this country more often when I hear them comparing our standard of living and that of other countries.
If they understood the wholeheartedness, courage, physical energy and strength with which our fathers devoted themselves to moulding Canada into the land we know, they would realize that this is a land of sacrifice if ever there was one. Even if they have slight grievances and feel that the people are not entirely satisfied, they should not dare to draw comparisons that always sound feeble, especially when the Canadian standard of living is set beside that of other countries.
It is good to be living in Canada, as those who have been elsewhere very well know. One need only look around to ascertain that I speak the truth when I say that Canada can be favourably compared with any other country in the world.
I	regret, Mr. Speaker, that I am short of time; however, I wish to congratulate again the hon. the treasurer on his patience. I beseech him to disregard all critics; let him think and act as a true Canadian, always bent on making Canada the happiest country in the world.
